#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151872 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/id #uuid "a18b37e2-4ab1-5fd1-b985-3021172abc41" :question/math-academy-id "e-6306" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\dfrac{y^3}{x^2} \\, \\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given by $y = x$ for $\\, 0 \\le x \\le 4$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x)$. Therefore, by setting $x=t$, we have\n\n$$\ny = y(t) = t\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 4\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t)j \\\\\n&= i + j \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{y^3}{x^2}\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= \\frac{t^{3}}{t^{2}} = t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)\\frac{y^{3}}{x^{2}}ds &= ∫_{0}^{4}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}t \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}∫_{0}^{4}tdt \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}t^{2} \\mid _{0}^{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(4^{2} - 0^{2}) \\\\\n&= 8\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151873 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e381c74c-43ed-5588-ba9a-4c7d4fb2caf3" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "b4f26e02-fb0f-5577-90f4-52e7eeabd857" :question/math-academy-id "e-6310" :question/problem "Consider the line integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} \\dfrac{y^2}{x} \\,\\textrm ds$, where $C$ is the section of the curve $y = \\dfrac{x^3}3$ between the points $(0,0)$ and $(3,9)$.\nWrite the line integral in the form $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x)$. Therefore, by setting $x=t$, we have\n\n$$\ny = y(t) = \\dfrac{t^3}{3}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\dfrac{t^3}3 \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 3\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{t^{3}}{3})j \\\\\n&= i + t^{2}j \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1^{2} + (t^{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + t^{4}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{y^2}{x}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= ((\\frac{t^{3}}{3})^{2})/(t) = \\frac{1}{9}t^{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)f(x, y)ds &= ∫_{0}^{3}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{9}t^{5} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 + t^{4}} \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9}∫_{0}^{3}t^{5}\\sqrt{1 + t^{4}}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d261248-985f-505e-85cb-4597828f95b2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Explicit Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df476a83-9a43-559a-a22e-d5e316d0e798" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(5\\sqrt{10} - \\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150907} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0809e4-5085-51c7-b1bc-a3ecc86ac5a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}(5\\sqrt{10} - \\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150908} {:answer/id #uuid "2947ac70-dd53-5663-a131-2e6877056f66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(5\\sqrt{10} - \\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150909} {:answer/id #uuid "5593042e-6da9-5e30-9c80-86791741e5f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150910} {:answer/id #uuid "79eb5ab5-ab77-5426-b900-782d708aa5fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd0809e4-5085-51c7-b1bc-a3ecc86ac5a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}(5\\sqrt{10} - \\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150908} :answer-field/id #uuid "094256cf-51df-5726-9725-6493c1dfd31e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150906}] :question/id #uuid "78a90830-8528-5d8e-9b99-56c7720a064a" :question/math-academy-id "q-76483" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x, y) = 2y^{2}$ along the curve $y = e^{x}$, $0 \\le x \\le \\ln 3$."}]}
